@* A term with an explanation available on demand.

   **This is not a `title` attribute, and that is the whole reason it is a component.** `title`
   never appears on a touch screen, is unreliable for keyboard users, cannot be styled, is
   announced inconsistently by screen readers, and cannot be dismissed. Every one of those fails
   the standard this app holds itself to elsewhere, and none of them is visible to a compiler or
   to a test that only checks the text is present somewhere.

   **It is supplementary and must stay so.** WCAG is explicit that information conveyed only by a
   tooltip is information some readers do not get. Nothing here may be the only place something is
   said — the tier cards still print their budget and their cap, the review step still prints every
   finding. This adds a sentence about a rulebook term next to the term; it does not replace one. *@

<span class="tip-wrap"
      @onmouseenter="Show"
      @onmouseleave="Hide">
    @* The trigger is a real button, which is what makes this reachable at all by keyboard and by
       touch: a tap focuses it, and focus shows the tip. A `<span>` with a mouse handler is a
       tooltip only for people using a mouse.

       aria-describedby rather than aria-label on the button, because the sentence is a
       *description* of the term and the button's own name is what the button does. Giving the
       button the sentence as its name would have a screen reader read the whole explanation
       where it expects "Help". *@
    <button type="button"
            class="tip-trigger"
            aria-describedby="@Id"
            aria-expanded="@(_shown ? "true" : "false")"
            @onfocus="Show"
            @onblur="Hide"
            @onkeydown="Key">
        <span aria-hidden="true">?</span>
        <span class="sr-only">What @Term means</span>
    </button>

    @* **The description and the visible tip are two elements, and that split is load-bearing.**

       The description is what `aria-describedby` names. It is always in the document, so the
       reference never dangles and a screen-reader user is given the sentence without having to
       hover for it — deliberately unlike the budget breakdown, where `aria-controls` is written
       only while its target exists because there the target genuinely does not.

       **It is `sr-only` rather than the visible box hidden, and the narrow harness is why.** The
       first version had one element hidden with `visibility: hidden`, which keeps its layout box
       — so an absolutely-positioned tip up to 22rem wide contributed real horizontal overflow at
       375px while closed, on every page carrying one. CI caught it; nothing local did. `sr-only`
       is a clipped 1px box and contributes none. *@
    <span id="@Id" class="sr-only">@Text</span>

    @* The visible copy, and only that: `aria-hidden`, or the sentence is announced twice — once
       as the button's description and again as text in the page.

       It sits inside the wrapper rather than beside the button so that moving the pointer onto
       the tip keeps it open. WCAG 1.4.13 asks for exactly that — hoverable, dismissable,
       persistent — and a tip that vanishes when you reach for it fails the first. *@
    <span class="@TipClass" role="tooltip" aria-hidden="true">@Text</span>
</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>The term being explained. Used in the trigger's accessible name.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Term { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The sentence. Supplementary — never the only place something is said.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Text { get; set; } = "";

    private bool _shown;

    private void Show() => _shown = true;
    private void Hide() => _shown = false;

    /// <summary>
    /// Escape dismisses it, which WCAG 1.4.13 requires and which the obvious implementation
    /// leaves out — a tip that can only be closed by moving the pointer is not dismissable by
    /// somebody who is not using a pointer.
    /// </summary>
    private void Key(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape") Hide();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// <b>Derived from the term rather than generated.</b> A fresh identifier per render would be
    /// enough to break the replay's strongest guard, which renders the same character twice and
    /// requires the two pages to be byte-identical — a random id differs every time and would
    /// report a difference that is not one. Two tooltips on one page explaining the same term
    /// would share an id, which is the one collision worth having: the description is the same
    /// sentence either way.
    /// </summary>
    private string Id => "tip-" + new string(
        Term.ToLowerInvariant().Select(c => char.IsLetterOrDigit(c) ? c : '-').ToArray());

    private string TipClass => _shown ? "tip shown" : "tip";
}
